Firmware libraries, set libs3 (round 6, frames track)
-----------------------------------------------------

The build images jumper/arduino-cli:1.5.1-avr1.8.8-libs3, jumper/arduino-runner:
1.5.1-avr1.8.8-libs3-r1 and jumper/arduino-pico:6.3.1-libs3-r1 (built by Docker
from toolchains/arduino/Dockerfile, services/runner/docker/Dockerfile and
toolchains/arduino-pico/Dockerfile; Jumper's repository and packages contain
none of these libraries) also preinstall:

  Adafruit GC9A01A 1.2.0           BSD License, Copyright (c) Adafruit Industries
  DHT sensor library 1.4.7         MIT License, Copyright (c) 2020 Adafruit Industries
  FastLED 3.10.8                   MIT License, Copyright (c) 2013 FastLED
  TFT_eSPI 2.5.43                  FreeBSD License, Copyright (c) 2023 Bodmer, with
                                   parts under the Adafruit BSD / MIT licenses
                                   (its license.txt; its GFXFF fonts come from
                                   GNU FreeFont via Adafruit GFX, see LIBRARIES.md)
  Adafruit NeoPixel 1.15.5         GNU Lesser General Public License 3.0,
                                   Copyright (c) Adafruit Industries

They are linked only into firmware that includes them. The test sketches in
packages/parts/fixtures/libs3/ are such firmware: the fastled_* images link
FastLED, the gc9a01a_* images Adafruit GC9A01A, the dht_* images the DHT sensor
library, tft_espi_xiao.hex TFT_eSPI. Adafruit NeoPixel is LGPL-3.0: a firmware
binary that links it and is given to someone else carries LGPL-3.0 duties
(offer the library's source and let the recipient relink the firmware with a
modified library, e.g. by providing the sketch's object files or source). So
Jumper commits no binary that links it: the neopixel_* sketches are built and
run only by the test, inside the build image, and Jumper never ships the
library itself.

The web app decodes colour screen frames with fflate 0.8.3 (MIT License,
Copyright (c) Arjun Barrett), the same package listed above for cases.

Talking devices (round 7, voice). Jumper ships no text-to-speech engine and no
DFPlayer library. The DFPlayer Mini part implements the module's serial frames
from its public manual; examples and hints use a ten-line frame helper written
for Jumper. The common DFRobotDFPlayerMini Arduino library states "GNU Lesser
General Public License" in its header: Jumper neither ships nor requires it.
In the browser the words of a clip are read aloud by the browser's own Web
Speech API voice. The factory pack's sd-card/make-clips.sh calls tools the
person installs themselves; they are not distributed with Jumper:
  piper       MIT License for https://github.com/rhasspy/piper (its newer
              continuation piper1-gpl is GPL-3.0); each voice model has its
              own licence
  espeak-ng   GNU General Public License 3.0 or later, run as a separate
              program (https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng)
  ffmpeg      LGPL 2.1 or GPL depending on the build (https://ffmpeg.org)

ESP32-family boards (round 9 spike, emulator)
---------------------------------------------

ESP32 / ESP32-S3 / ESP32-C3 firmware runs in Espressif's fork of QEMU
(https://github.com/espressif/qemu, tag esp-develop-9.2.2-20260417), GNU General
Public License 2.0. Jumper does not link QEMU: it runs as a separate program in
the Docker image built by toolchains/qemu-esp/Dockerfile, which compiles that
tag with Jumper's patch toolchains/qemu-esp/0001-jumper-bridge.patch (GPL-2.0,
like the files it modifies; it adds a GPIO/UART/I2C bridge). Anyone receiving
the image can rebuild it from the Dockerfile, the public tag and the patch.
packages/sim-backends/src/qemu-esp talks to it over a socket (Apache-2.0).

ESP32 firmware (packages/sim-backends/fixtures/esp32*.merged.bin.gz and
anything built with toolchains/arduino-esp32) is built with:

  arduino-esp32 core 3.3.12        GNU Lesser General Public License 2.1,
  (esp32:esp32)                    Copyright (c) Espressif Systems and contributors
  ESP-IDF v5.5.5 (precompiled in   Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) Espressif Systems
  the core) and its components     (each component under its own licence; see
                                   the core's tools/esp32*-libs)
  esptool 5.3.1 (build tool only)  GNU General Public License 2.0, run as a program

The JumperEth Arduino library (toolchains/arduino-esp32/libraries/JumperEth)
contains ESP-IDF v5.5.5 components/esp_eth/src/openeth (esp_eth_mac_openeth.c,
openeth.h, esp_openeth.h), Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2019-2024 Espressif Systems
(Shanghai) CO LTD, built into the sketch because the precompiled core leaves
CONFIG_ETH_USE_OPENETH off; the rest of the library is by the Jumper Authors.

STM32 and nRF52 boards in Renode (round 9 spike)
------------------------------------------------

The Renode backend (packages/sim-backends/src/renode) runs boards in a separate
process, the Docker image built from toolchains/renode/Dockerfile:

  Renode 1.17.0                    MIT License, Copyright (c) 2010-2026 Antmicro
                                   (https://renode.io; the image contains the
                                   upstream portable release unmodified; its
                                   bundled third-party licenses are under
                                   /opt/renode/licenses in the image)

packages/sim-backends/src/renode/external-control.ts speaks Renode's
ExternalControl protocol, written from the MIT-licensed client in Renode's
tools/external_control_client. No Renode code is linked into Jumper.

Firmware fixtures (packages/sim-backends/fixtures/renode/*.elf) are built with
toolchains/arduino-stm32 (STM32duino core 2.11.0: LGPL-2.1 core, ST HAL / CMSIS
BSD-3-Clause / Apache-2.0) and toolchains/arduino-mbed-nano (arduino:mbed_nano
4.4.1: LGPL-2.1 core, Arm Mbed OS Apache-2.0), plus newlib / libgcc as above.

